Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(ArgBind);
define
    abCaller=7;
    abOther=7;
    abElse=41;
    abRebinds=0;
    abRead   code={ return argument; };
    abWrite  code={ argument = 5;          return argument; };
    abSet    code={ argument := abElse;    return argument; };
    abArrow  code={ argument <- abElse;    return argument; };
    abRun code={
        b1 = abRead(abCaller);
        print "B1 read through the argument       " b1 " (want 7 -- the passed field)":;
        abWrite(abCaller);
        print "B2 caller after `argument = 5`     " abCaller " (want 5 -- a WRITE reaches the caller)":;
        print "B3 untouched sibling               " abOther " (want 7 -- ANTI-VACUITY)":;
        abSet(abCaller);
        print "B4 caller after `argument := x`    " abCaller " (want 5 -- REBIND REFUSED, unmoved)":;
        abArrow(abCaller);
        print "B5 caller after `argument <- x`    " abCaller " (want 5 -- REBIND REFUSED, unmoved)":;
        print "B6 the rebind target itself        " abElse " (want 41 -- never became the caller)":;
        };
    ;
abRun();
print "ARGBIND SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

argBindT -- TRY-AND-BUY B.  `argument` is a BINDING, not a holder: a bare
mention is the passed field, a WRITE through it reaches the caller, and a
REBIND of the binding refuses by name.

Minimal delta from incant/argWriteT (same shape, same caller/sibling pair),
plus the two rebind spellings the ruling names.

WHY B2 AND B4 ARE BOTH PINNED AT 5, and it is the whole point of the file:
they assert OPPOSITE mechanisms with the same number.  B2 wants the write to
LAND (5, changed from 7); B4 wants the rebind to be REFUSED so the caller is
UNMOVED (still 5).  B2 is what makes B4 non-vacuous -- without a preceding
write that moved the value, "unmoved" would be satisfied by a run in which
nothing whatever happened.

B3 and B6 are the anti-vacuity siblings.  B3 says the write was not a
broadcast; B6 says the refused rebind did not quietly half-fire and leave the
rebind target holding the caller.

THE REFUSAL IS PRESENCE-WITH-VALUE, NOT ABSENCE (rule H4): the run prints
`ARGUMENT REBIND REFUSED` on stderr, twice, once per spelling, naming the
operator.  A check that merely asserted "the caller did not move" would go
green the day the refusal is deleted and the rebind silently does nothing.

NEGATIVE CONTROL (rule H7), recorded rather than implied: with the refusal
block in runOP removed and the binary rebuilt, B4 and B5 read 41 -- the rebind
lands and the caller becomes the rebind target.  That is the run that must go
RED for this file to certify anything.
